WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.143
- [Narrator] Inside this
270,000 gallon tank,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.130
live over 600 animals, ranging from sharks

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.940
to tiny fish.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.100
National Aquarium in Baltimore brings in

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.950
around 1.3 million visitors a year.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.980
But what guests don't
always see is what goes on

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.960
to keep all these animals alive,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.330
from turning salt pallets into seawater

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.430
to training the fish.
- I kinda have to be

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.020
a Jedi with this.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.210
- [Narrator] To scrubbing
the unique 3000 piece

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.070
replicated coral reef system,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.830
National Aquarium's total operations add

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.596
up to $45 million a year.

00:00:37.596 --> 00:00:40.140
(kids chattering)

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.120
The Blacktip Reef exhibit was renovated

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.870
with $12.5 million and $800,000 went

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.450
to this entire replicated
coral reef system.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.400
So maintaining this expensive habitat

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.960
is a top priority.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.980
Three times a week,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.870
scuba certified divers gear up

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.310
to clean the exhibit.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.400
- In total, when you
look at this equipment

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.410
and you add a mask to it,

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.570
it can be about $2,000
to outfit one diver.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.490
- [Narrator] These acrylic
pads originally used

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:09.840
for car washing also work
on the viewing windows.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.030
- [Allison] Since we're
very buoyant in the water,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.640
we use suction cups to
help keep us anchored

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.810
to the window.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.730
One of the most time consuming tasks

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.710
we have is hand scrubbing

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.280
because the algae growth
is regularly occurring

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.470
and we have these larger utility brushes

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.140
that are good for an overall area.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.060
We have these awesome grout brushes

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.040
that really help into getting into some

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.870
of those smaller areas and better

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.640
on more textured areas.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.680
And these little
toothbrushes are really great

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.630
when it comes to brushing

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.910
some of those more detailing areas.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.890
- [Narrator] This cast
coral costs anywhere

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:47.223
from $25 to $1,700 a piece.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.970
Animals are used to the divers like Zoe,

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.860
who gets close because she's curious

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.330
about the bubbles they create.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.070
But not all animals in the exhibit

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.140
are considered safe so the divers have

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.510
to constantly be aware
of their surroundings.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.160
- [Diver] We do have
some hazardous animals

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:03.240
in there with spines.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.340
- [Narrator] And dive cleaning
is just the first step

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.830
in quality control when
it comes to the water.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:08.970
- [Allison] The next line of defense

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.613
behind us is the water filtration systems.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.720
- So all the animals in Blacktip Reef

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.020
are complete relying on
this set of equipment

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.430
that we're looking at.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.050
- [Narrator] This exhibit's
life support system

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.970
is monitored 24X7.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.470
- We actually have two people

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.690
at all times any given night.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.120
- [Narrator] First, this
de-nitrification system

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.070
helps remove toxic nitrates,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.840
which creates healthy living conditions

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.310
and mitigates algae growth.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.710
A second filter backwash
process takes water

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.480
from the exhibit, filters it

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.830
before pumping it back.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.830
This system prevents
roughly 2.7 million gallons

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.990
of water from being discarded annually.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.010
- [Andy] That's a lot of sea water

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.080
that we would be throwing away.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.220
It's very expensive.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.360
- [Narrator] With this system,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.670
the aquarium only needs
to replace a portion

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.830
of Blacktip Reef's water at a time,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.690
and instead of sourcing seawater,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.340
the aquarium makes its own

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.830
with these 2,500 pound salt pallets.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.390
- [Andy] The animals
that we are supporting

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:06.090
in these habitats have learned to survive

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.290
in that vast full
compliment of seawater ions.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.610
If any of them are missing,

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:16.200
it can throw that internal
process off balance.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.580
- [Narrator] It takes
eight of these pallets

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.830
to create a batch of replicated seawater,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:20.663
which can be used

00:03:20.663 --> 00:03:23.160
for all the aquarium's seawater exhibits.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.800
And thanks to Blacktip
Reef filtration systems,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.320
the exhibit only needs
around 20,000 gallons

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.210
of new seawater every three months,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.993
saving the aquarium
roughly $450,000 a year.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.010
To fill this tank with living animals,

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.730
the aquarium had a
budget of about $325,000.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.030
Some of these fish cost nearly $8,500 each

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.500
to ship from Australia.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.640
But in the past,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.080
some of the predator species

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.730
have eaten the smaller fish,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.050
which is why it is crucial

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.360
to keep the apex predators well fed.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.730
- [Worker] Pepe is our
black-blotched stingray

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.563
- [Narrator] And trained.

00:03:57.563 --> 00:04:00.210
- He is quite rambunctious.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.620
Oh, Pepe.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.470
- [Narrator] The exhibit
spends about $21,000 a year

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.360
on feed for the animals.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.280
Feed varies by species ranging

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.193
from mackerel and squid
to krill and pellets.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.400
And in total, the Blacktip Reef goes

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.670
through about 6,700
pounds of feed in a year.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.880
The eight sharks in this
exhibit eat 30% of that.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.830
- We're gonna be working with Zuri today

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:25.290
so she'll be receiving her food, her diet

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.703
during the training session.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.460
- [Narrator] This buoy
guides Zuri, the shark

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.570
on where to swim and this
orange target signals

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.160
where to stay.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.790
- It took Zuri a few
months to learn to target

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.470
and to really follow the target.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.870
Nice, nice.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.270
She was very curious about the platform,

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.240
but staying nice and still in calm

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:49.800
is a bit of a challenge for her.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.960
- [Narrator] Aquarius
also use feeding sessions

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.210
to train an animal for
routine health checks.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.810
Other animals that go
through training sessions

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.510
include the pack of Blacktip Reef sharks,

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.880
Australian whiptail rays,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.370
Zoe, the other zebra shark,

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.683
and Bertha, the Queensland grouper.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.160
She's still working on it.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.170
- We use positive reinforcement training

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.060
in order to help us do as many things

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.740
with them as we can

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.540
with as little stress as possible.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.870
The more stress an animal
experiences, of course,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.750
the more negative health ramifications

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:25.583
that can have.

00:05:25.583 --> 00:05:26.970
- [Narrator] Predators get fed separately

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.430
from the rest of the
fish and during feeding,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.950
Aquarius will monitor for unusual behavior

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.170
of all 630 animals.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.030
- There is the chance that we would need

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.220
to move a fish to a different exhibit,

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.500
for instance, if they are not getting

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.080
along with another fish in the habitat,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.910
we might need to separate the two fish.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:48.090
Or perhaps they are eating things

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.590
that might cause them harm.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.780
- [Narrator] Some animals
like Duncan, the puffer fish

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.210
were relocated because
they were eating the coral

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.280
which didn't cause any medical issues

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.230
but did damage the reef.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.850
Duncan will likely be replaced

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.020
with other creatures

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.210
which will continue the
aquarium's conservation

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.770
and education initiatives.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.140
- Adding animals into
the Blacktip Reef habitat

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.760
is something that typically takes place

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:09.960
about once a year.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.410
- [Narrator] The National
Aquarium generated $430 million

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.620
in tourists related
economic impact in 2022

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.110
and new residents could
bring in more guests.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:19.943
- [Stephanie] Guests who have come

00:06:19.943 --> 00:06:21.630
to know the Blacktip brief habitat

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:23.190
for a long time.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.620
New Calypso, who was a green sea turtle

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.130
although we were devastated
to lose her in 2020,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:31.200
we are excited at the prospect

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.310
of hopefully being able

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.750
to add another green sea turtle

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.553
into Blacktip Reef in the future.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.933
(gentle music)

